  "title": "A cleanup error can now leave the live direct child running",
  "detail": "On a cleanup error, terminate used to SIGKILL the directly spawned child unconditionally (\"ours even if process discovery is unavailable\"). With a cleanup scope it now first reads the child's birth and records it, and skips child.kill() when that read throws or returns null, or when an earlier ownership write failed. On darwin processBirth throws \"Native process inspection is unavailable\" without the native module, which is exactly the discovery failure the old comment covered, so the child keeps running (and can keep writing to the checkout); a failed first write also leaves no recovery row. The live direct child (exitCode and signalCode both null) can't have a reused PID, so it must always be killed: record it first when its identity can be read, but never let a failed read or write prevent the kill, and keep that failure in the termination error. Better still, capture and persist the direct child's identity right after spawn so it is recorded before any cleanup starts. Change the new test that currently asserts the child survives so it asserts the child is killed."
 },
