  "title": "On Linux a record from before a reboot can match and SIGKILL an unrelated process",
  "detail": "Linux processBirth returns only the boot-relative start time in clock ticks (/proc/<pid>/stat field 22). process_cleanups rows survive a reboot (and a rollback followed by a later upgrade), and after a reboot PIDs and start ticks repeat, so an unrelated process with the same PID and start tick passes the identity check and gets SIGKILL. That breaks \"never signal a process you did not start\". Persist the boot identity with each record (Linux /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id; on macOS the birth is absolute, but recording kern.boottime keeps one rule) and retire records from another boot without signalling. The table is new in this PR, so change it in place. Add a test where a record from another boot matches a live process's PID and birth and is never signalled."
 },
