  "title": "Recovery waits on zombies and kills one member at a time",
  "detail": "Recovery SIGKILLs one recorded member, then polls processBirth until it changes, for up to 10 s, before touching the next member. On Linux processBirth still returns the start time for a zombie (/proc/<pid>/stat exists), so a killed member whose parent is another recorded member that is still SIGSTOPped (frozen by the interrupted sweep, not yet killed) stays a zombie: recovery times out with \"Recorded process still alive\", stops, and leaves the frozen parent and its rows behind. Live cleanup already treats zombie state as gone (stillRunning). SIGKILL every confirmed member of the invocation first, then wait for all of them under one deadline, using the same zombie-aware liveness check as live cleanup. Add a Linux-path test with a stopped recorded parent and a recorded child where the child's row comes first."
